<article class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-10 space-y-10 text-slate-300">
  
  <!-- 1. What is profit margin? -->
  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
      What is Profit Margin?
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Profit margin is a financial metric that reveals the exact percentage of total revenue your business retains as clean profit after covering project expenses. While top-line revenue shows how much money flows through your business, profit margin measures how efficiently you turn that revenue into actual take-home income.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      For freelancers, consultants, and independent agencies, tracking profit margins ensures that project pricing leaves enough room for operating overhead, taxes, and net business growth. A high revenue figure can be deceiving if heavy contractor expenses or software costs swallow most of the payout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. How to calculate profit margin -->
  <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-800/80 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
      How to Calculate Profit Margin Step-by-Step
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Calculating your project or business profit margin involves four quick steps:
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2.5 text-sm text-slate-300 font-medium">
      <li><strong class="text-white">Determine Gross Revenue:</strong> Total dollar amount billed to your client for the work.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Sum Direct Project Costs (COGS):</strong> Add up all expenses directly required to complete the job (e.g. subcontractor fees, stock assets, third-party software licenses, raw materials).</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Calculate Net Profit:</strong> Subtract direct costs from gross revenue (<span class="font-mono text-indigo-300">Revenue - Cost = Net Profit</span>).</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Divide Net Profit by Revenue:</strong> Divide the net profit dollar figure by your gross revenue, then multiply by 100 to get your profit margin percentage.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Profit margin formula box -->
  <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-800/80 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
      Profit Margin & Markup Formulas
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-300">
      Use these formulas to calculate net profit, profit margin percentage, and product markup:
    </p>

    <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-950 border border-indigo-500/30 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-4 shadow-inner">
      <div class="border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
        <span class="text-slate-400 block text-[10px] uppercase font-sans tracking-wider mb-1">1. Net Profit Formula</span>
        <span class="text-white font-bold">Net Profit = Gross Revenue - Total Costs</span>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
        <span class="text-slate-400 block text-[10px] uppercase font-sans tracking-wider mb-1">2. Profit Margin Formula</span>
        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">Profit Margin (%) = [ (Gross Revenue - Total Costs) / Gross Revenue ] × 100</span>
      </div>

      <div>
        <span class="text-slate-400 block text-[10px] uppercase font-sans tracking-wider mb-1">3. Markup Percentage Formula</span>
        <span class="text-sky-400 font-bold">Markup (%) = [ (Gross Revenue - Total Costs) / Total Costs ] × 100</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. Worked numeric example -->
  <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-800/80 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
      Worked Practical Example
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      Suppose a freelance brand designer quotes a fixed-fee website overhaul for <strong class="text-white font-mono">$2,500</strong>. To fulfill the contract, the designer hires a freelance copywriter for <strong class="text-white font-mono">$700</strong> and purchases premium stock photos and font licenses for <strong class="text-white font-mono">$300</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        <span class="text-slate-400">Gross Project Revenue:</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-white font-bold">$2,500.00</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        <span class="text-slate-400">Total Expenses ($700 + $300):</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-rose-400 font-bold">$1,000.00</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        <span class="text-slate-400">Net Profit ($2,500 - $1,000):</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold">$1,500.00</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        <span class="text-slate-400">Profit Margin ($1,500 / $2,500 × 100):</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-emerald-400 font-extrabold text-base">60.00%</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-between pt-1">
        <span class="text-slate-400">Markup ($1,500 / $1,000 × 100):</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-sky-400 font-bold">150.00%</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 italic">
      Result: For every $100 earned on this project, the freelancer retains $60 in clean net profit after paying third-party expenses.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. When should freelancers use this? -->
  <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-slate-800/80 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
      When Should Freelancers Use a Profit Margin Calculator?
    </h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-sm">1. Quoting Fixed-Price Contracts</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Before sending a proposal, calculate whether your estimated project fee covers all subcontractor support while leaving your target margin.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-sm">2. Handling Discount Requests</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          When clients negotiate for a discount, check your minimum threshold to avoid taking on unprofitable work that damages cash flow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-sm">3. Reviewing Monthly Retainers</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Audit ongoing monthly client retainers to confirm that scope creep or unexpected software costs haven't eroded your profit margins.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-sm">4. Setting Productized Service Rates</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          When packaging services (e.g. monthly SEO audits or design passes), structure pricing to guarantee consistent 60%+ margins.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. 5-Question FAQ -->
  <section class="space-y-6 border-t border-slate-800/80 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
      Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">
          1. What is a healthy profit margin for freelancers and solo business owners?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Service-based freelancers typically aim for a <strong>50% to 80% gross profit margin</strong>, because service businesses have lower physical inventory costs. Product-based businesses or agencies with high payroll costs usually operate around 15% to 35% net profit margins.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">
          2. What is the difference between Profit Margin and Markup?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Profit Margin measures profit as a percentage of total revenue, whereas Markup measures profit as a percentage of cost. For example, if an item costs $50 and sells for $100, the profit is $50. The profit margin is 50% ($50 / $100), but the markup is 100% ($50 / $50).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">
          3. Why does my profit margin drop when I hire subcontractors?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Subcontractor fees are direct cost items (COGS). If you pay a developer $1,000 on a $2,000 client project without increasing your overall fee, your profit drops to $1,000 and your margin shrinks from 100% to 50%. Always mark up contractor costs when building client estimates.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">
          4. Should I include my own hourly wages in the project cost?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In solo freelancing, your labor is part of your net profit draw. However, if you run a formal agency or plan to delegate tasks, you should factor labor wages into project costs so your business margin accurately reflects company profitability beyond owner pay.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 space-y-2">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-white text-base">
          5. Can profit margin ever exceed 100%?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          No. Because profit margin is calculated as a fraction of total revenue (<span class="font-mono">Profit / Revenue</span>), profit margin can never exceed 100%. Markup, on the other hand, can easily exceed 100% or 200% if your selling price is more than double your cost.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</article>
